-- title:
--   THEOREM 2, p. 228 — nonstationary Poisson arrivals: V̄(t) → V(∞) w.p. 1 iff Z(t) → V(∞) w.p. 1
-- statement:
--   **PASTA for nonstationary Poisson arrivals.**
--
--   Let $(\Omega,\mathcal F,P)$ be a probability space and $N$ a process with values in a measurable space. Let $A$ be a Poisson process with intensity $\lambda(t)$, where $\lambda(t)\ge0$ is bounded and integrable on every $[0,t]$, and the limit
--
--   $$\lim_{t\to\infty}t^{-1}\Lambda(t)=\bar\lambda,\qquad0<\bar\lambda<\infty,\qquad\Lambda(t)=\int_0^t\lambda(s)\,ds,$$
--
--   exists. Let $B$ be a set with $\{N(t)\in B\}$ measurable for every $t\ge0$ and $U(t)=\mathbf 1\{N(t)\in B\}$, with paths left continuous with right-hand limits w.p. 1, jointly measurable on $\Omega\times[0,\infty)$, and assume lack of anticipation with respect to the joint history: for each $t\ge0$, $\{A(t+u)-A(t),u\ge0\}$ is independent of $\mathcal F_t$, the σ-field generated by $\{A(s),U(s);0\le s\le t\}$. Define the arrival rate weighted time average
--
--   $$\bar V(t)=\Lambda(t)^{-1}\int_0^tU(s)\lambda(s)\,ds,$$
--
--   and $Z(t)=Y(t)/A(t)$ as before. Then for every random variable $V(\infty)$,
--
--   $$\bar V(t)\to V(\infty)\ \text{w.p. 1}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad Z(t)\to V(\infty)\ \text{w.p. 1},\qquad t\to\infty.$$
--
--   With a time-varying rate, arrivals see the time average weighted by the arrival rate; with a constant rate this is Theorem 1.
--
--   **Formalization Note** "Bounded" is read as $\lambda(s)\le C$ for $s\ge0$ (with $\lambda\ge0$), and "integrable" as integrable on every bounded interval $[0,t]$; only nonnegative times are constrained. $\bar V(t)=0$ when $\Lambda(t)=0$, which does not affect the limit. Lack of anticipation is assumed against $\mathcal F_t$ rather than against $\{U(s),0\le s\le t\}$ alone as in the printed LAA (p. 225). The page's proof of (6) uses this joint independence ("from LAA and the independent-increments property … for any set in $\mathcal F_t$"), and the printed LAA together with independent increments does not give it: with $U=1$ on $[0,1]$, $U=G$ on $(1,2]$, $U=0$ afterwards, where $G$ is the parity of $A(2)-A(1)$ masked by a fair bit computed from the arrival times in $[0,1]$, $G$ is independent of the future increments after time $1$, so the printed LAA holds, yet $E\{R(2)-R(1)\mid R(s),s\le1\}=\pm\lambda e^{-2\lambda}\ne0$ on an event of positive probability. The joint condition implies the printed one.
-- source:
--   Wolff, Poisson Arrivals See Time Averages, Oper. Res. 30 (1982), DOI 10.1287/opre.30.2.223, p. 228, §3, THEOREM 2 and the assumptions preceding it

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_WolffPASTA_Main_Setting

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology

namespace WolffPASTA.Main.Supplement

/-- THEOREM 2 (Wolff 1982, §3, p. 228). For a Poisson process with bounded, integrable rate `λ(t)`
and `Λ(t)/t → λ̄ ∈ (0, ∞)`, under LAA, `V̄(t) → V(∞)` w.p. 1 if and only if `Z(t) → V(∞)` w.p. 1.
Lack of anticipation is taken against the joint history `ℱₜ` (`LAAJoint`), the independence the
page's proof of (6) uses; the printed LAA (future increments independent of the past of `U` only)
does not imply (6) or LEMMA 1 (see the Formalization Note). -/
theorem theorem_2
    {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    {S : Type*} [MeasurableSpace S] (N : ℝ → Ω → S)
    (A : ℝ → Ω → ℕ) (rate : ℝ → ℝ) (hA : IsPoissonProcessWithIntensity P A rate)
    (hrate_nonneg : ∀ s, 0 ≤ s → 0 ≤ rate s)
    (hrate_bdd : ∃ C : ℝ, ∀ s, 0 ≤ s → rate s ≤ C)
    (hrate_int : ∀ t, 0 ≤ t → IntervalIntegrable rate volume 0 t)
    (lbar : ℝ) (hlbar : 0 < lbar) (hlim : Tendsto (fun t => Lambda rate t / t) atTop (𝓝 lbar))
    (B : Set S) (hB : ∀ t, 0 ≤ t → MeasurableSet {ω | N t ω ∈ B})
    (hpath : PathRegular P (U N B))
    (hjoint : JointlyMeasurable (U N B))
    (hLAA : LAAJoint P A (U N B))
    (Vinf : Ω → ℝ) :
    (∀ᵐ ω ∂P, Tendsto (fun t => Vbar (U N B) rate t ω) atTop (𝓝 (Vinf ω))) ↔
      (∀ᵐ ω ∂P, Tendsto (fun t => Z (U N B) A t ω) atTop (𝓝 (Vinf ω))) := by sorry

end WolffPASTA.Main.Supplement
